course: dictionary
lesson: 13972
title: Not bad: litotes
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Sometimes people say something good by saying that its opposite is not true. This is called litotes. «The test was not easy» means the test was hard. Litotes is a kind of understatement: it makes something sound smaller than it really is.
explain: Look for a word like not or no, followed by an opposite. «She is not unkind» means she is kind. «It is not a small problem» means it is a big problem. The two negatives, like not and un-, turn back into a positive meaning.
explain: Why do people use it? It can sound modest, polite, dry or a little funny. «That was not a bad goal, Sam» is a friendly way of saying it was a good goal. Ask yourself: what does the speaker really mean?
fact: Litotes is a figure of speech: a way of using words for a special effect.
fact: Litotes is a kind of understatement, where you make something sound smaller than it is.
fact: «Not bad» usually means good, and «not unhappy» means quite happy.
fact: Litotes is very common in British and Irish speech, and it appears in old poems such as Beowulf.
fact: Litotes is a noun. It is the same word in the plural.
exercise: Tom says, «That film was not bad at all.» What does he mean? | It was very long | *It was good | It was in colour | It was terrible
exercise: «It is not a small problem.» What does this really mean? | It is a small sum of money | It is a tiny problem | There is no problem at all | *It is a big problem
exercise: Which sentence is an example of litotes? | *«She is not unkind to animals.» | «The dog ran fast.» | «I am very tired.» | «The sun is shining brightly.»
test: Mum says, «Your drawing is not unpleasant.» What does she mean? | It is drawn in pencil | *It is quite nice | It is very big | It is awful
test: Why might a speaker choose litotes? | *To sound modest, polite or dry | To make a long story even longer | To sound plain and rude | To stay silent
exam: Which word means a kind of understatement where you say a thing by saying its opposite is not true? | *Litotes | Simile | Hyperbole | Onomatopoeia
